# Kandazhi: Destruction of Thought!

Many books have appeared in the past, written by scholars of differing kinds and expressing many different views about the doctrines and conceptions of God held by ancient Tamils. Now another book has appeared, entitled *The Doctrines of the Brahmo Samaj and the Doctrines of the Ancient Tamils*, written by Pulavar M. Rasamanickam, B.O.L.

Though only twelve pages long, the booklet explains such subjects as the conception of God, opposition to caste, unity, this world and the next, the removal of evil, the duties of householders, and husband and wife. Through these explanations the author appears to have made it his duty to identify the doctrines of the ancient Tamils with those of the modern Brahmo Samaj.

He first says: “The Brahmo Samaj is founded upon the Upanishads. Brahman is the supreme reality; it is neither male nor female, is present everywhere, and is omnipotent. This is the Brahmo Samaj conception of God, and it was also that of the ancient Tamils.” He cites Tamil works from the *Tolkappiyam* through the Eight Anthologies.

Everyone accepts that the *Tolkappiyam* is the foremost and most ancient work of the Tamil people. Yet it contains none of the invocations to God found in later books. It became customary for an author, before beginning a book, to worship either his chosen god so that it might be completed properly or a god appropriate to the excellence of its subject. Why is no such invocation found here? Had anything corresponding to the meanings modern interpreters attach to the word “God” existed in Tolkappiyar’s age, and had invoking such a god before every undertaking truly been customary, Tolkappiyar would have done so. He did not. Why? Plainly because no such thing existed. He evidently considered that asserting the existence, at the very opening of his work, of something nonexistent and unnecessary would be improper and reveal profound ignorance.

In that condition, can there be truth in the author’s assertion that the *Tolkappiyam* and other Tamil works held the Upanishadic conception of God?

There may perhaps be truth in his statement that the Brahmo Samaj is based upon the Upanishads. I do not wish to investigate how far it is true; those who accept and practise Brahmo Samaj doctrines may examine it. But one cannot refrain from condemning an author who borrows Sanskrit Upanishads to establish Tamil doctrine. His belief that the doctrines of the ancient Tamils cannot be explained without the Upanishads and Brahmo Samaj is deeply pitiable. It is unclear why modern Tamils, already acquainted with ancient Tamil thought, should require the Brahmo Samaj for that purpose.

The author also says that the Brahmo Samaj opposes image worship. As evidence he cites the words `கொடிநிலை`, `கந்தழி`, and `வள்ளி` from the *Purathinai Iyal* of the *Tolkappiyam*, declaring that these alone sufficiently prove that Tamils of Tolkappiyar’s age did not worship images.

Many researchers place Tolkappiyar four thousand years ago. Even if that date is not accepted, no one can deny the antiquity and primacy of the *Tolkappiyam*. What can one say of an author who marvels that the Brahmo Samaj, born only yesterday, reveals to us a non-iconic conception of God already stated in the most ancient primary work of the Tamils?

The *Tolkappiyam*, said to have taught Tamils a non-iconic conception of God thousands of years ago, remains their primary ancient text. Yet is there even the smallest connection between that doctrine and the beliefs of Tamils today? How many people in Tamil Nadu now follow a conception without image worship? Tamil religious belief has reached a point beyond any requirement to learn from the *Tolkappiyam* or other books: wood and bronze, stone and mountain, tree and anthill, dog and pig, crow and eagle, lizard and snake, and innumerable other things are worshipped by descendants of those supposedly non-icon-worshipping people. Why did this pitiable condition arise?

If Tolkappiyar’s non-iconic conception was truly Tamil doctrine, why did it never enter practice? Why did the esoteric teaching of a man regarded as one of the earliest accomplished sages fail to help humanity? If this teaching were true and necessary, no age, person, thing, or cause could have damaged or destroyed it.

I welcome the intelligence of changing doctrines with changing times, whether they concern God or impose even sterner restrictions. But that explanation does not fit here. The author proclaims a non-iconic ancient Tamil doctrine while standing within an inescapable prison inhabited by people committed to image worship. How can this be called adaptation to changing times? In our country the change has proceeded upside down.

What benefit comes from shouting that non-iconic belief is superior and was Tolkappiyar’s doctrine after image worship has been planted in the people’s minds? Is the person shouting at least himself opposed to image worship? Since the author does not condemn its practitioners, we must conclude he is not. I have seen many like him change their doctrines according to circumstance.

The people of Tolkappiyar’s own country did not accept the doctrine attributed to him; instead, they adopted beliefs imported by outsiders. Tamils made the god Pillaiyar, imported from Vatapi, an object of worship. Why did they not accept the conception supposedly given by their own incomparable poet Tolkappiyar? Close examination suggests that Tolkappiyar said nothing about God at all, which is also why he wrote no invocation.

Some may ask what `கந்தழி` means if it does not explain a conception of God in the *Tolkappiyam*. But it cannot be denied that Nachinarkkiniyar, not Tolkappiyar who used the word, conferred divinity upon it. Nachinarkkiniyar lived when Aryan concepts of God had spread through Tamil Nadu. Efforts to propagate a doctrine of God in a country that did not need it would inevitably meet resistance, and many stratagems would be used to overcome that resistance and establish it.

Nachinarkkiniyar explains `கந்தழி` as “that which stands without attachment and transcends all categories.” No one would suppose that a god based on this explanation could trouble anyone. Even if Tamils of his time accepted it, there need have been no harm: something attached to nothing, possessing no form, seeking no assistance or support, and transcending every category, whether called God or anything else, could do us neither good nor evil.

Yet even Tolkappiyar could not have dreamed that this very word `கந்தழி` would become a scorching fire consuming Tamil life today. My concern is that people like this author, who take pride and thrill at Nachinarkkiniyar’s explanation, should at least now recognize how greatly this `கந்தழி` torments Tamils.

The author further says ancient Tamils called God `இறை` (*irai*). He explains `இறைமை` as leadership, that which possesses leadership, the supreme reality, the entity above and underlying all created things. But this raises a question: was the Tamil god the unattached `கந்தழி`, or an `இறைவன்` possessing attachment and responsibility?

The author himself introduces attachment by granting `இறை` the office of leadership. A king who rules a country is called `இறைவன்`; likewise, the god said to create, preserve, and destroy the world is called `இறை` or `இறைவன்`. As a king bears attachment and responsibility toward his people, a god governing the whole world must do likewise. Anyone accepting leadership must possess concern for those beneath it. Thus the author’s own meaning, “that which holds leadership,” compels the question: unattached Kandazhi, or attached Lord?

He next says that the name `இறை` later became the qualitative names beauty, youth or Murugu, and auspiciousness or Sivam. The god first called Kandazhi became Irai, then Azhagu, Murugu, and Sivam, and has now become stone, bronze, and wood. I trust the author recognizes this wonder. He and others should consider how far the ancient Tamil conception has “advanced” and what its present stone, bronze, and wooden form will become in future.

We cannot omit noting that Nachinarkkiniyar gave Tolkappiyar’s `கந்தழி` a meaning mixed in an Aryan mould; it then became `இறை`, later acquired elephant and six-faced forms, and finally caused the destruction of thought among modern Tamils descended from supposedly non-iconic ancestors.

The author writes: “When man attempted to make God masculine like himself, he added the masculine suffix `-an` to those qualitative names and called it Iraivan, Azhagan, Murugan, and Sivan.” No one can fail to praise a person undertaking the mighty task of making masculine something that lacked masculinity! But here “masculine” does not mean courage; it means changing something described as neither male nor female into a male.

Neither the person who masculinized genderless Brahman nor the author explaining and approving it seems to have recognized that the act contradicts the very definition of that God. If omnipotent Brahman knew the form it ought to possess and made itself neither male nor female, a worshipper’s alteration of it into a male must surely be condemned by believers in that doctrine, even if I do not condemn it.

Just as a man masculinized the god he worshipped and named it Iraivan, Azhagan, Murugan, and Sivan, could a woman not feminize her god and call it Iraivi, Azhagi, Murugi, and Sivai? The author records no such development. Some religious texts use Irai and Sivai not to say women feminized God, but as husband and wife: a god said to be neither male nor female is divided into both and the two are married. This too appears to be an “advance” in ancient Tamil theology!

The truth implied when the author says “man made God masculine” is that women possessed no right even to worship God. What does it mean that a man masculinized a deity said to be common to all life, supreme, and beyond sex? There is no difference between that and saying, “The Lord is God to me; I am God to you; therefore worship me.” Proverbs such as “Even if he is stone, he is the husband,” “Even if he is grass, he is the man,” and “To a wife, her husband is God” rest upon this idea.

Leave those proverbs aside and see what the *Tirukkural*, praised as Tamil scripture, universal scripture, and the word that never lies, says:

> She worships no god but rises worshipping her husband;  
> When she says “Rain,” rain will fall.

Rather than investigate how true or reasonable it is that rain obeys a woman who worships her husband instead of God, wisdom requires us to see how low and degraded women’s position was considered in Valluvar’s age. Should writers concerned with modern Tamil progress not consider how intelligent it is to publish such explanations while seeking national liberation?

I need not condemn the author’s intention to demonstrate similarities between ancient Tamil and Brahmo Samaj doctrines. But an author publishing a book must ask whether its ideas suit the book and the people living in the time and country where it appears. He must retain what deserves acceptance, reject what deserves rejection, and frame his ideas according to the condition of his contemporaries.

To reinforce Valluvar’s view, the author cites an incident involving Kannagi. On the night before Kovalan returned after leaving Madhavi, Kannagi reportedly had a frightening dream and told her Brahmin companion. Her friend proposed temple worship as expiation. Kannagi smiled, shook her head, and replied, `பீடன்று` (*peedanru*). The author interprets this as “not an honour for women of good family,” meaning temple worship was not the duty of respectable women.

The same author who declares there was no talk of caste in ancient Tamil Nadu rejoices in describing high and low lineage among ancient Tamils. If caste did not exist, could caste-created lineage have existed? Why he uses Kannagi’s response as support for Valluvar’s idea demands investigation.

Why do scholars who attribute sanctity to temples support the claim that respectable women should not enter them? What reason can there be except that the temple’s “sanctity” would spoil women’s purity? Nor can one suppose that no respectable woman of Kannagi’s time visited temples. Kannagi herself had evidently worshipped there repeatedly; otherwise her companion would not have invited her to do so.

If Kannagi had no custom of temple worship, her friend would never have invited her. The invitation plainly shows that the friend knew Kannagi went to temples whenever she suffered such distress. Those who understand the relationship between a heroine and her confidante will not deny it.

The author supports Kannagi’s supposed view that temple worship is wrong for respectable women. Does he therefore regard all women who worship in temples today as respectable, or as women outside respectable lineage? I leave that decision to him. I believe it is the attitude that no ancient Tamil text may be altered or corrected, whatever faults it contains, that makes this author and others speak so.

Finally, I shall briefly examine what he says about ancient Tamil love-marriage and chastity.

Once a man and woman join through mutual understanding and marry for love, there should be no separation, rupture, or disagreement between them. Yet the author displays his own conception of love-marriage by citing a couplet in which a man says to his wife, “I must go out on some business and shall return.” She replies, “I cannot consent to your departure. Tell those other women who can survive awaiting your return that you are going and will come back.” Valluvar expresses it thus:

> If there is a way not to leave, tell me; otherwise speak  
> Of your swift return to those who will live until then.

This conversation reveals what ancient Tamil love-marriage was supposedly like. What lesson does the couplet teach except that, after marrying a woman, a man must never leave her for even a second at any time or for any reason? Consider whether that can suit this age or any age. If a husband never leaves his wife and remains seated upon her lap, how is their life to proceed?

Moreover, if a woman who married through mutually understanding love does not know her husband’s mind, suspects him, and says, “I cannot consent to your departure,” consider the quality of that supposedly harmonious love-marriage. Using this incident, the author asks, “What would you think of a wife who speaks so?” People would surely answer: “Such a wife has become blind through her boundless love for her husband.”

22-7-1945

---NOTES---
The Tamil OCR source is retained verbatim in the bilingual document. `கந்தழி` (*Kandazhi*) is a disputed technical word in the *Tolkappiyam*; it is transliterated because the essay itself contests Nachinarkkiniyar’s theological gloss, “that which stands without attachment and transcends all categories.” The English title preserves the Tamil wordplay between `கந்தழி` and `கருத்தழிவு` (“destruction of thought”). The source author’s degree is OCR-damaged and is conservatively rendered as B.O.L. based on the visible Latin letters. Quotations from the *Tirukkural* are translated for meaning. Historical claims about the authorship of Sangam literature, Vatapi Pillaiyar, gender, caste, and ancient Tamil identity are translated as the essay’s claims without independent endorsement.
